.\" Read it without installing:   man -l man/taggle-rock.1
.\" Install for your user:        ln -s "$PWD/man/taggle-rock.1" ~/.local/share/man/man1/
.\" tests/test_man_page.py checks every option in the scripts is listed here.
.TH TAGGLE-ROCK 1 2026-09-15 "taggle-rock 0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
taggle-rock \- tag private free-text notes with a local language model
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY "python3 tag_run.py"
.OP \-\-limit N
.OP \-\-since YYYY\-MM\-DD
.OP \-\-dry\-run
.OP \-\-show
.OP \-\-retag
.OP \-\-trial
.OP \-\-model NAME
.OP \-\-notes\-from RUN_ID
.OP \-\-config FILE
.OP \-\-vocab FILE
.YS
.SY "python3 tag_run.py undo"
.I RUN_ID
.YS
.SY "python3 draft_vocab.py"
.OP \-\-limit N
.OP \-\-min\-notes N
.OP \-\-config FILE
.OP \-\-vocab FILE
.YS
.SY "python3 review_vocab.py"
.RB [ review | summary ]
.OP \-\-min\-notes N
.OP \-\-draft FILE
.OP \-\-vocab FILE
.YS
.SY "python3 spot_check.py"
.RB [ grade | summary ]
.OP \-\-run RUN_ID
.OP \-\-sample N
.OP \-\-same\-notes\-as RUN_ID
.OP \-\-config FILE
.OP \-\-vocab FILE
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B taggle-rock
reads free-text notes, asks a language model running on this computer
which tags from your vocabulary fit each one, and sends the tags back to
the tracker, so a search for
.I joint pain
finds a note that only said
.IR "ankles killing me today" .
.PP
Four promises hold throughout:
.IP \(bu 3
The note text is the record. It is only ever read, never changed.
.IP \(bu
Tags are derived from the text. Every tag records the run that wrote it,
so a bad run can be taken back out in one go.
.IP \(bu
The model is not trusted. Its answer is held to a JSON schema of your
vocabulary, then checked again by rules. Its confidence is never asked
for, because it means nothing.
.IP \(bu
Notes go only to the model on this computer (Ollama on localhost).
Run logs hold dates, boxes and tags, never note text. Note text is shown
only when you grade notes by hand in
.BR spot_check.py ,
and vocabulary drafts quote short phrases; both stay in files only you can
read.
.PP
The expensive fuzzy reading happens once, in a batch, and later searches
use the stored tags cheaply.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B tag_run.py
Tag every note that is new or edited since it was last tagged. Tags go to
the tracker in batches of 20, so stopping with Ctrl\-C keeps what was done.
Prints a run id, and writes
.IR runs/RUN_ID.jsonl .
.TP
.BI "tag_run.py undo " RUN_ID
Remove every tag one run wrote.
.TP
.B draft_vocab.py
Have the model read every note and suggest words and tags, then write
.I drafts/vocab_draft.yaml
for you to read, edit and copy from.
.I vocab.yaml
itself is never changed. Answers are cached per note, so Ctrl\-C and
running it again carries on. Suggestions are checked: a phrase that is not
really in the note is thrown out, known words are skipped, and a tag the
tracker would refuse is dropped.
.TP
.BR "review_vocab.py " [ review ]
Go through the draft's suggestions, most common first, and write
.IR vocab.yaml .
Each one can be added (y), rejected for good (n), skipped (s), renamed (r),
moved to another category (c), merged into a tag you already have (m), or
have its words edited (w). q saves and stops. Decisions go to
.I drafts/decisions.jsonl
as you make them, so running it again carries on, and a rejected suggestion
is not offered again.
.B It shows phrases from your notes,
so use its own terminal window.
.TP
.B review_vocab.py summary
How many suggestions are decided and how many are left. Counts only.
.TP
.BR "spot_check.py " [ grade ]
Show you a sample of a run's notes with their tags, in your terminal, and
record whether each tag is right and what is missing. The sample is fixed
for a run, so q and running it again carries on.
.B Run it in its own terminal window,
not with
.B !
inside Claude Code: it shows note text.
.TP
.B spot_check.py summary
Print the run's totals and the grading so far: tags marked right, notes
missing something, and whether each missing item was in the vocabulary
(the model missed it) or not (a vocabulary gap). Counts only, so it is
safe to share.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS tag_run.py
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " N"
Tag at most
.I N
notes.
.TP
.BI \-\-since " YYYY\-MM\-DD"
Only notes from that date on.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Ask the model but send nothing and write no log.
.TP
.B \-\-show
Print each note's tags (never its text).
.TP
.B \-\-retag
Tag again every note not yet tagged with the current vocabulary, whether it
had tags or not. Use it after changing
.IR vocab.yaml .
Each note keeps its old tags in search until its new ones arrive, so
search is never left empty. Stopped with Ctrl\-C, running it again skips
the notes already done with this vocabulary.
.TP
.B \-\-trial
Ask the model and write the run log, but send nothing: the tags on the
tracker are left alone.
.B spot_check.py
grades a trial log like any other run, so two models can be compared on the
same notes without touching what search uses.
.TP
.BI \-\-model " NAME"
Ask this Ollama model instead of the usual one. The run log and the tracker
record which model tagged each note.
.TP
.BI \-\-notes\-from " RUN_ID"
Exactly the notes you graded for that run, tagged already or not: naming
them is the selection, so
.B \-\-retag
is neither needed nor consulted. With
.B \-\-trial
and
.BR \-\-model ,
that is a like-for-like comparison on notes you have already judged.
.SS draft_vocab.py
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " N"
Read at most
.I N
notes.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-notes " N"
List a suggested tag only if it came up in
.I N
or more notes. Default 2, which hides one-off noise.
.SS review_vocab.py
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-notes " N"
Only offer suggestions that came up in
.I N
or more notes. Default 2. A high number is a good first pass: it puts the
tags that would change search the most in front of you first.
.TP
.BI \-\-draft " FILE"
The draft to review. Default
.IR drafts/vocab_draft.yaml .
.SS spot_check.py
.TP
.BI \-\-run " RUN_ID"
The run to grade. Default: the newest run log with notes in it.
.TP
.BI \-\-sample " N"
How many notes to grade. Default 30. A bigger number keeps the notes
already graded and adds more.
.TP
.BI \-\-same\-notes\-as " RUN_ID"
Grade the notes that were graded for an earlier run, with this run's tags:
a fair before and after. Ignores
.BR \-\-sample .
.SS tag_run.py, draft_vocab.py and spot_check.py
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
The tracker connection. Default
.IR config.json .
.SS All commands
.TP
.BI \-\-vocab " FILE"
The vocabulary. Default
.IR vocab.yaml .
.SH WORKFLOW
.nf
.RS
draft_vocab.py ──► review_vocab.py ──► tag_run.py --retag
      ▲             (writes vocab.yaml)         │
      │                                         ▼
      └──── vocabulary gaps ◄──── spot_check.py, then summary
.RE
.fi
.PP
Grade the same notes after each change with
.BR \-\-same\-notes\-as ,
so the numbers compare.
.SH VOCABULARY
Copy
.I vocab.example.yaml
to
.I vocab.yaml
to start. It
has a
.B version
and four categories:
.BR body_part ,
.BR symptom ,
.BR severity " and"
.BR context .
Under each, one tag per line, followed by the words that mean it:
.PP
.EX
version: 1
body_part:
  joints: [knuckles, wrists, knees, ankles]
symptom:
  fatigue: [tired, wiped out, exhausted]
.EE
.PP
A tag is lowercase ASCII letters and digits, with spaces, hyphens,
slashes or apostrophes inside, 1 to 48 characters. Bump
.B version
when a change alters what an old tag meant; adding words or tags does
not need it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I config.json
server, client_id, secret and user_id for the tracker. Gitignored.
.TP
.I vocab.yaml
Your vocabulary. Gitignored and readable only by you: it fills up with
words from your own notes. Start it with
.BR "cp vocab.example.yaml vocab.yaml" .
.TP
.I vocab.example.yaml
A starting vocabulary to copy. Replace it wholesale for notes that aren't a
health diary; only the shape matters.
.TP
.I runs/RUN_ID.jsonl
One line per note: date, box, the note's hash, the vocabulary version,
tags and the tracker's result. No note text.
Readable only by you.
.TP
.I runs/RUN_ID.grades.jsonl
Your spot\-check marks and what you typed as missing. No note text.
Readable only by you.
.TP
.I drafts/vocab_draft.yaml\fR, \fPdrafts/cache.jsonl
The vocabulary draft and the model's cached answers. They quote words
from your notes. Readable only by you.
.TP
.I drafts/decisions.jsonl
What you decided about each suggestion. Readable only by you.
.TP
.I vocab.yaml.bak
The vocabulary as it was before
.B review_vocab.py
last wrote it.
.TP
.I systemd/taggle-rock.service\fR, \fPsystemd/taggle-rock.timer
A nightly run at 04:30 that catches up after sleep and only runs on mains
power. Copy to
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/
to use.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The model is
.B qwen-local
through Ollama at
.IR http://localhost:11434 .
Both are set in
.IR qwen.py .
Ollama must be running:
.BR "systemctl \-\-user start ollama" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done, including a run stopped with Ctrl\-C or q.
.TP
.B 1
The tracker, the vocabulary or a file could not be read or reached. The
message says which.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was wrong.
.SH EXAMPLES
Try tagging on five notes, sending nothing:
.PP
.EX
python3 tag_run.py \-\-dry\-run \-\-limit 5 \-\-show
.EE
.PP
Before and after a vocabulary change:
.PP
.EX
python3 spot_check.py \-\-run run\-20260915\-093312
python3 tag_run.py \-\-retag
python3 spot_check.py \-\-run NEW_RUN_ID \-\-same\-notes\-as run\-20260915\-093312
python3 spot_check.py summary \-\-run NEW_RUN_ID
.EE
Try another model on the notes you have already graded, without changing
any tags:
.PP
.EX
python3 tag_run.py \-\-trial \-\-model qwen\-instruct \-\-notes\-from run\-20260915\-093312
python3 spot_check.py \-\-run TRIAL_RUN_ID \-\-same\-notes\-as run\-20260915\-093312
python3 spot_check.py summary \-\-run TRIAL_RUN_ID
.EE
.SH BUGS
Without
.BR \-\-retag ,
a changed vocabulary does not make
.B tag_run.py
tag unchanged notes again; it only looks for new or edited notes.
.PP
.B undo
removes a run's tags but does not bring back the tags notes had before,
so undoing a
.B \-\-retag
run leaves its notes untagged until the next run.
.PP
Run logs written before
.B \-\-retag
existed have no vocabulary version, so the first retag redoes every note.
.PP
A run log does not know if a later run tagged the same note again, so
grade a run before tagging again.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ollama (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1)
.PP
.I PLAN.md
in the taggle-rock directory: the design, decisions and measured speeds.
